## Tangent Space to the Grassmannian

**Claim.** The tangent space to the Grassmannian $G(k, V)$ at a point $E$ (a $k$-dimensional subspace of the $n$-dimensional vector space $V$) is canonically isomorphic to
$$T_E\, G(k, V) \;\cong\; \operatorname{Hom}(E,\, V/E).$$

---

### Proof

Let $\dim V = n$ and fix a $k$-dimensional subspace $E \subseteq V$, so $E \in G(k,V)$. We work over a field (e.g. $\mathbb{R}$ or $\mathbb{C}$); the argument is field-independent.

**Step 1 — A chart around $E$.** Choose a complementary subspace $F \subseteq V$ so that $V = E \oplus F$ (hence $\dim F = n - k$). Every $k$-dimensional subspace $E'$ sufficiently close to $E$ is the graph of a unique linear map $\varphi : E \to F$. Concretely, writing the projection $V \to E$ along $F$ as $\pi_E$, the subspace $E'$ is transverse to $F$ (i.e. $\pi_E|_{E'} : E' \to E$ is an isomorphism) precisely when $E'$ lies in the open chart $U_F$ around $E$, and then
$$E' = \{e + \varphi(e) : e \in E\}$$
for a unique $\varphi \in \operatorname{Hom}(E, F)$. This gives the standard coordinate identification
$$U_F \;\xrightarrow{\;\sim\;}\; \operatorname{Hom}(E, F), \qquad E' \longmapsto \varphi,$$
with $E$ itself corresponding to $\varphi = 0$.

**Step 2 — The tangent space in coordinates.** Since $E$ corresponds to the origin in the vector space $\operatorname{Hom}(E, F) \cong \mathbb{A}^{k(n-k)}$, the tangent space at $E$ in this chart is simply
$$T_E\, G(k,V) \;\cong\; \operatorname{Hom}(E, F).$$

**Step 3 — Independence of the complement (canonical form).** The identification $T_E G(k,V) \cong \operatorname{Hom}(E,F)$ appears to depend on the choice of complement $F$. We remove this dependence. The quotient map $q: V \to V/E$ restricts on $F$ to an isomorphism $F \xrightarrow{\sim} V/E$ (since $V = E \oplus F$). Composing,
$$\operatorname{Hom}(E, F) \;\xrightarrow{\;q_*\;}\; \operatorname{Hom}(E, V/E), \qquad \varphi \longmapsto q \circ \varphi,$$
is an isomorphism of vector spaces. Transporting the identification of Step 2 through it yields
$$T_E\, G(k,V) \;\cong\; \operatorname{Hom}(E, V/E).$$

It remains to check this is independent of $F$. Take another complement $F'$, with quotient isomorphism $q'_*: \operatorname{Hom}(E,F') \xrightarrow{\sim} \operatorname{Hom}(E,V/E)$. The change of chart $\operatorname{Hom}(E,F) \to \operatorname{Hom}(E,F')$ sends $\varphi$ to the unique $\varphi'$ whose graph is the same $E'$; equivalently $\varphi' = p_{F'} \circ (\mathrm{id}_E + \varphi) \circ (\mathrm{id}_E)^{-1}$ where $p_{F'}$ is projection to $F'$ along $E$. At $\varphi = 0$ (the point $E$) its differential is the linear map $\varphi \mapsto p_{F'}|_F \circ \varphi$. Under the quotient isomorphisms,
$$q'_* \circ (p_{F'}|_F \circ -) = q \circ - = q_*,$$
because $q' \circ p_{F'}|_F = q$ (both sides send $f \in F$ to its class in $V/E$, since $V = E \oplus F' = E \oplus F$). Hence the two transported identifications $T_E G(k,V) \to \operatorname{Hom}(E,V/E)$ agree at the origin. So the isomorphism $T_E G(k,V) \cong \operatorname{Hom}(E, V/E)$ is canonical — independent of the chosen complement.

**Step 4 — Dimension.** As a consistency check,
$$\dim T_E\, G(k,V) = \dim \operatorname{Hom}(E, V/E) = (\dim E)(\dim V/E) = k(n-k),$$
which equals $\dim G(k,V)$, as expected.

**Step 5 — Intrinsic interpretation (optional but clarifying).** A tangent vector at $E$ is an equivalence class of smooth curves $E(t)$ in $G(k,V)$ with $E(0) = E$. Given such a curve, choose (locally) a smooth family of bases $\{e_1(t),\dots,e_k(t)\}$ of $E(t)$ with $e_i(0) = e_i$ a basis of $E$, and define
$$\delta e_i = e_i'(0) \in V.$$
The map $\Phi : E \to V/E$, $\Phi(e_i) = [\delta e_i] \in V/E$, is well defined (independent of the choice of moving basis: changing $e_i(t)$ by a $t$-dependent invertible matrix $A(t)$ with $A(0) = I$ alters $\delta e_i$ by elements of $E$, which vanish in $V/E$). This $\Phi \in \operatorname{Hom}(E, V/E)$ is precisely the tangent vector, and in the chart $V = E \oplus F$ it recovers the linear map $\varphi$ of Step 1. This gives the canonical identification directly, without choosing a complement.

---

Therefore the tangent space to the Grassmannian $G(k, V)$ at the point $E$ is canonically
$$\boxed{\,T_E\, G(k, V) \;\cong\; \operatorname{Hom}(E,\, V/E)\,}.$$

